## [2] MATHEMATICAL PLAN (worked out in detail, self-audited)
Key discoveries from the analysis:

(A) CAPTURE THEOREM (new; closes GAP #1 unconditionally, no genericity/analyticity).
    Machinery: the generalized-gradient flow is replaced by ANY solution of the differential
    inclusion  Φ' ∈ -conv U(Φ),  U(x) = unit initial vectors of minimizing geodesics x→X
    (usc, compact; Filippov/Aubin–Cellina existence). Two facts:
      (i) off Σ := M^g_X (≥2 minimizing geodesics) the inclusion is single-valued, |Φ'|=1 and
          d_X grows at rate exactly 1 (first variation), so the margin
          m(t) = d_X(Φ_t) − d(p,Φ_t) is NONDECREASING while the flow avoids Σ
          (d(p,·) is 1-Lipschitz — rate ≤ 1);
      (ii) hence either the flow meets/accumulates at Σ at T ≤ diam − d_X(p) with
          m(T) ≥ d_X(p) > 0  (p reconstructed by an M^g center, quantitative margin), or it
          avoids Σ forever, d_X(Φ_t) = d_X(p) + t → ∞ — impossible on compact M ("escape").
    IMPORTANT subtleties handled: Σ is NOT closed (focal/conjugate degenerations); the argument
    only needs hitting times or approach times t_k ↓ T (margin continuity finishes both cases).
    Focal "stall" points (unique geodesic, in closure(Σ)\Σ, e.g. ellipse evolute, ellipsoid
    conjugate cusp) are just regular points of the inclusion — F' = 1 passes straight through.
    Checked against: ellipse-evolute example, ellipsoid cut-segment + cusp, S^n singleton.
    Consequences: compact M ⇒ R^g_X = M\X for EVERY closed X ≠ ∅ (even |X| = 1 — strengthens
    the |X|≥2 statements); Lieutier margin caveats deleted.
    NOTE (self-audit): margin m and even the quadratic F² − d² are NOT monotone through
    multi-geodesic arcs (explicit 3-point R² counterexample built) — that is why stopping at Σ
    is the correct formulation, and why the old "constant-margin" route could not work.

(B) HOROFUNCTION CHARACTERIZATION (new; closes GAP #2 structurally, and GAP #3 by redefinition).
    M complete ⇒ proper (Hopf–Rinow) ⇒ horofunction compactification M̄ = M ∪ ∂_h M exists for
    EVERY complete M (no curvature). Call h ∈ M̄ *defective* if h = lim [d(·,a_k) − d_X(a_k)]
    (locally uniformly) with a_k ∈ M^g_X. Then:
      SWALLOW (curvature-free, general): {h < 0} ⊆ R^g_X for every defective h — proof is three
      lines from local-uniform convergence; NO anchoring, NO pinching, NO offset condition.
      CHARACTERIZATION: R^g_X = ⋃ {h < 0 : h defective} for every complete M and closed X.
      (⊆ is definitional via finite h = d(·,a) − r(a); ⊇ is the swallow.)
      The R3 offset caveat dissolves: defectiveness is defined by the limit of the medial balls
      themselves, not by the Busemann function of the ideal endpoint; an offset-c drift is
      simply defective at level c, reconstructing {b_ξ < −c} — now a statement, not a gap.
    TRICHOTOMY (general): captured / drift-swallowed / escaping-unswallowed, specializing to
    R3's hull/drift/ray-escape on Hadamard and to "no escape" on compact M.
    Also noted: ideal defective h DOES fire on noncompact sec>0 (paraboloid bisector drifts);
    consistent with capstone since {h<0} is unbounded free but NON-convex there (b concave).

(C) WRITE-UP: new final note medial_axis_complete.tex — "the unconditional theorem on complete
    Riemannian manifolds": capture theorem + general swallow + characterization + trichotomy +
    specializations recovering Bia/R2/R3/Sph + updated 2×2 + remaining open (faithful M^g-free
    certificates off the known geometries; quantitative shielding threshold).
    Then surgical patches: Sph rem:containment + summary-table "(analytic)" row; Tri
    rem:twogeo-caveat + compact-row wording; R3 pointer-remark on the offset caveat.
    Compile all PDFs.

